50,3,1,talk,May,,"Candidate A looks promising given the old pilot's success, but Shizue mentioned data agreement issues.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,Candidate D is a nightmare for the rosters. We have no one left to cover the late turns.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,3,1,talk,Midori,,"Candidate B addresses the mid-city slowness, but May's research shows lane-priority often fails.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"Candidate C involves the pre-war shells, which I've found are full of unpredictable voids and high costs.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Candidate A would reduce those high contractor fees I found, provided the data issues can be solved.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,2,talk,May,,"@Shizue, if the ferry and rail offices can't agree, is Candidate A dead in the water?","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,I'm leaning toward Candidate A or B. D is simply not feasible with current staffing.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,2,talk,Midori,,"If B's dedicated lanes are like the previous failed ones, maybe A is the safer bet for efficiency.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"Yes, the lack of common data terms makes A very difficult to implement quickly.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,But A solves the long-term financial drain of the multiple ticketing contractors.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,3,talk,May,,"Let's reconsider B. If we focus on the boarding islands rather than just lanes, it might be more stable.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Jonathan, would the lapsed grant cover the infrastructure for B?","{""words"":10,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,3,talk,Midori,,I still think B addresses the actual time loss passengers are experiencing mid-city.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,3,talk,Shizue,,B avoids the pre-war shells and the ferry-rail data conflict. It seems the most pragmatic.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"The capital ledger could likely support B, and it avoids the operating subsidy cuts.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,4,talk,May,,I agree. B is a balanced choice that avoids the pitfalls of the other three.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,I'm on board with B. It's manageable compared to the staffing disaster of D.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,4,talk,Midori,,I vote for B. It targets the mid-city delays.,"{""words"":9,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,4,talk,Shizue,,I also vote for B. It's the safest path forward.,"{""words"":10,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I concur. B is the most fiscally and operationally sound option.,"{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
